{"author":{"id":"78f9c13831d7e4cd23da41f1921a0df29d82a91276573361524e3bf648269089"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787745868,"nonce":"cDKFYKLlCVZmh56yZYMFPitxLuY=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOOEv6_s8AAAABOVQDGg","github-url":"https://github.com/graphcore-research/gwr/issues/364","origin":"github"},"title":"Validate links in rustdoc output","message":"Currently links within the API documentation produced with rustdoc are not validated, and a quick check has uncovered multiple issues:\n\n```\nlychee --offline --include-fragments=none target/doc\n508210/508210 ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ Finished extracting links                                                                                                              1/1 ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ Finished searching for alternatives                                                                                                              Issues found in 70 inputs. Find details below.\n\n...8\u003c...\n\n🔍 508210 Total (in 1s 269ms) 🔗 121287 Unique ✅ 263066 OK 🚫 135 Errors 👻 245009 Excluded\n```\n\nIdeally we'd catch these broken links when running tests locally. A rough outline for how this might be done is:\n\n1. Serialise the tests in gwr-developer-guide to ensure that the current test has already run `cargo doc`.\n2. Add a test which runs `lychee` on the build rustdoc output.\n\nThere is an issue around the use of anchor links within the rustdoc output that requires further understanding. Running lychee with our standard config (i.e. `--include-fragments=full`) results in many errors reported with the links to pages containing Rust source.","files":null}]}